# tgloggerd webapi

The Go web service of tgloggerd. The web backend moves from the Drogon
application in web/ to this service, one feature at a time
(docs/design/web-go.md). While both run, nginx sends /api/ here and
everything else to Drogon. Both read the same databases with the same
users and grants.

So far it serves a health endpoint and the sign-in endpoints described
under Authentication:

    GET /api/health   200 {"status":"ok","checks":{"ro":"ok","app":"ok"}}
                      503 when a pool does not answer; that pool's check
                      reads "unavailable" and the reason is logged.

The health endpoint needs no login and exists for probes and for
checking the routing.

It also serves the files, /files/<token>, described under Files, and
the pages of the React application, described under Pages.

And the live updates, GET /api/events, described under Live updates.

And the chat windows and the entity summaries, GET
/api/chats/{scope}/{id}/messages and GET /api/entities, described under
Chats.

And the advanced search, GET /api/search/{entity}, and the chat
search of the pickers, GET /api/chats/search, described under Search.

And the administration: forwarding, Join chat, "Look up real name"
and the MCP administration, with their pages and those of the
accounts and federations, described under Administration.

And the MCP endpoint, /mcp, described under MCP.

And account email: verified addresses, set-password and verification
links, forgotten passwords and security notices, described under
Account email.

# The API contract

openapi.yaml (OpenAPI 3.1) is the contract of the service's API: every
route served is described there, with its status codes, headers and
bodies, and the React application generates its client from it
(web/app, npm run gen:api). The sections below explain the behaviour;
should they and the description ever differ, that is a bug in one of
them, fixed in both at once.

Operations designed but not served yet are in the description too,
marked `x-tgd-status: planned`: the transfer of forwarding
configurations. A port serves the operation and drops the marker in
the same change. A per-account
bearer-token scheme for clients without cookies is reserved there
(apiToken) and planned as well.

internal/server/openapi_test.go compares the description with the
route table (Server.routes in internal/server/server.go). It fails when
a served route is not described, when a described operation is not
served and not marked planned, or when a served one is still marked
planned. Every route goes into that table; a route registered anywhere
else escapes the check. The test reads the YAML with a small reader of
its own (the standard library has none), which accepts only the layout
the file's header comment describes.

# Authentication

Sessions, CSRF tokens, file tokens, password hashes and the daemon's
bearer key are made exactly as the Drogon web makes them
(web/src/auth, web/src/daemon/Api.cpp), from the same WEB_APP_KEY, so a
session started on either service is valid on the other, a password
change on either ends every session on both, and a /files/<token> link
minted by one opens in the other. Tests check this against values the
Drogon code produced (internal/auth/testdata).

The session is the signed cookie tgw_session. Each request with one is
checked against web_users.session_epoch, as Drogon's AuthFilter does,
and against is_active, which Drogon does not check (disabling an
account bumps the epoch as well, so it ends there too). The role and
the forced password change are read from that row, not from the
cookie, so a change applies to the next request.

Each session also has a row in web_sessions, keyed by the SHA-256 of
its cookie value (decisions/2026-11-18-sessions-list.md), read in the
same query as the account. A sign-in here records it with the device
(User-Agent) and address; a session signed in by the Drogon web, or
before the records existed, is recorded the first time it reaches
this service. A request records the last use and address once a
minute at most. A session whose row is ended (signed out) is refused
by both services, so POST /api/logout ends a copied cookie too. As
sessions are told apart by their cookie, a sign-in here picks an
expiry no other session of the account has (one second earlier per
clash), and with sessions that never expire the expiry is the end of
the year 9999 instead of 0.
State-changing requests carry a CSRF token in the X-CSRF-Token header:
for a signed-in request the token GET /api/session or POST /api/login
answers, which is the same token the Drogon pages put in their forms;
for POST /api/login a pre-login token from GET /api/login, which also
sets it as the cookie tgw_csrf (path /api/login) to be sent back.

Errors are {"error": "<code>"}, with "message" (the Drogon login page's
text) and a fresh pre-login "csrf" token on refused logins.

    GET /api/login    200 {"signed_in":false,"csrf":"<login token>"}
                      and the tgw_csrf cookie; {"signed_in":true}, with
                      no token, when the request carries a signed
                      session cookie.

    POST /api/login   X-CSRF-Token: <login token>
                      {"username":"...","password":"..."}
                      200 {"user":{"id":1,"username":"admin",
                      "role":"admin"},"csrf":"<session token>",
                      "landing":"/"} and the tgw_session cookie;
                      landing is the account's landing page, where a
                      sign-in without a next page goes.
                      403 csrf: the token or its cookie is missing or
                      wrong. 400 bad_request: the body is not JSON.
                      429 rate_limited: WEB_LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS failures
                      for this address and username, or
                      WEB_LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS_PER_ACCOUNT for the account
                      from all addresses, within WEB_LOGIN_WINDOW.
                      401 invalid_credentials: no such
                      account, a disabled one, or a wrong password.
                      Checked in that order; web_audit gets login_ok,
                      login_fail or login_ratelimited, as from Drogon.

    POST /api/logout  X-CSRF-Token: <session token>
                      204 and the session cookie cleared; the session's
                      record ended; a logout row in web_audit. 403
                      csrf. Other sessions of the account stay signed
                      in.

    GET /api/session  200 {"user":{...},"csrf":"<session token>",
                      "landing":"..."}.
                      401 unauthenticated: no valid session (a revoked
                      cookie is cleared). 503 unavailable: the session
                      could not be checked against the database; the
                      cookie is kept.

    POST /api/account/password
                      X-CSRF-Token: <session token>
                      {"current_password":"...","new_password":"..."}
                      200 {"csrf":"<new session token>"} and a new
                      session cookie; every other session of the
                      account ends. 400 wrong_password, weak_password
                      (under 8 bytes, or unchanged), bad_request. 429
                      rate_limited after repeated wrong current
                      passwords.

The user object also carries must_change_password: the account signed
in with a password set for it (typed by its creator, or a temporary
one). Until it has chosen its own, every operation behind a session
answers 403 password_change_required, except GET /api/session, POST
/api/logout and POST /api/account/password (the gate of the request
scope; see The request scope). There is no gate on a missing email
address (see Account email).

The accounts administration (GET and POST /api/accounts, GET and PATCH
/api/accounts/{id}, and its disable, enable, password-reset,
resend-invite and sign-out-everywhere actions) is described in
openapi.yaml and web/docs/accounts.md: who manages whom comes from the
request scope, a new account gets the first password its creator typed
or a temporary one shown once (or, asked for, an invitation by email),
a reset sends a link to a verified address when mail is set up and
gives a temporary password otherwise, and the last active superadmin
cannot be disabled or demoted. The federations editor (POST
/api/federations, PATCH and DELETE /api/federations/{id}) is the
superadmins' and is described there too.

The login throttle is in the process's memory, as in Drogon, so each
service counts failures on its own. The address logged and throttled
on is the connection's peer, as in Drogon.

# Files

    GET /files/<token>   a file's bytes, from the store or fetched from
                         Telegram through the daemon

The same URL, status codes and headers as the Drogon web's
MediaController, which it replaces; web/docs/media.md describes the
behaviour for both. The route is public, as there: the token is the
encrypted file id (internal/auth/filetoken.go), and a token that does
not open answers 404 like an unknown id. In short:

  - A stored file (after alias_of, one hop) is sent from WEB_STORAGE_DIR
    by http.ServeContent: Range (206, 416, and several ranges as
    multipart/byteranges), If-Modified-Since, If-Range, Last-Modified.
  - Anything else is fetched through the daemon (POST
    /v1/files/{id}/fetch): a "stored" answer is then served like a
    stored file, a "partial" one from TDLib's cache under
    <TG_DATA_DIR>/tdlib/ with the file's full size in Content-Range.
  - Without a session only files under TG_MAX_STORE_FILE_SIZE are
    fetched (403 "Sign in to load this file" otherwise); without
    WEB_TGLOGGERD_API nothing is (404 "File not stored").
  - Files are sent with Cache-Control: public, max-age=604800, and their
    ids are reported to the daemon's POST /v1/files/access once a
    minute (internal/daemon/access.go); a signed-in request writes a
    "media" row to web_audit.
  - Errors are small HTML pages, since a browser following a link or
    loading an image gets them, not the JSON API.

Who may see which file is decided by the request scope,
Server.requestScope (internal/server/media.go), passed to the one
query that looks the token's file up (logdb.GetFile). That query does
not filter yet: the token is the authorization, signed in or not
(see The request scope).

A session counts only when it is valid, epoch included, as for the
API; one that cannot be checked (a database error) counts as none, and
the request is served as an anonymous one. The Drogon web checks only
the cookie's signature and expiry here, so a session ended by a
password change can still stream big files there, not here.

## Routing /files/ through nginx

While Drogon serves /files/, nothing changes. To move it, add a
location for the prefix next to the /api/ one (web/README.web), with
the same upstream:

    location /files/ {
        proxy_pass         http://10.0.88.7:8081;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header   Connection        "";
        proxy_set_header   Host              $host;
        proxy_set_header   X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        # A fetch waits up to TG_FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECS plus 10 seconds for
        # the daemon; the default 60 s covers the default 30.
        proxy_read_timeout 120s;
        # Stream big files through instead of spooling them to disk.
        proxy_buffering    off;
    }

nginx passes Range and the conditional headers on by default. The
service logs, audits and throttles logins by the request's client:
the first X-Forwarded-For hop, read from the right, that is not in
WEBAPI_TRUSTED_PROXIES (nginx, and the CDN in front of it). Left
empty, that is the connection's peer, nginx, as in the Drogon web. The
Drogon route stays in place, so moving back is
removing the location. Both services read the same store, the same
TDLib directory and the same .env (WEB_STORAGE_DIR, TG_DATA_DIR,
TG_MAX_STORE_FILE_SIZE, TG_FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECS), and the webapi
container mounts the repository at /workspace and runs from there, as
the daemon does, so the paths the daemon answers with open in it.

# Live updates

    GET /api/events?topics=<list>&last=<seq>
                      text/event-stream, for as long as it stays open

Pages update as things happen (docs/design/web-live.md,
docs/design/web-live-client.md). The daemon publishes what changes on
its GET /v1/events (docs/internal-api.md); the service holds ONE
subscription to it and fans it out to every browser, so the daemon's
own subscriber limit is never reached. internal/live has the hub and
the source; the daemon's stream is the only source today, and a broker
(several web instances, several publishers) would be another
live.Source, leaving the fan-out as it is.

  - A session is required (401 without one). Topics: chat:<chat_id>
    (message.new, message.edited, message.deleted, file.stored of one
    chat), messages (the same for every visible chat), user:<id>
    (user.updated), group:<chat_id> (group.updated), stats
    (stats.changed). Repeats count once; more than 2000 topics, an
    unknown topic or a malformed last is 400 bad_request.
  - Framing: "retry: 3000" first, then one frame per event: "id:" the
    seq, "event:" the kind, "data:" the event's JSON (with seq). The
    first event is hello (the events after its seq follow) or resync
    (refetch what is shown). ": keepalive" every 15 seconds.
  - Resuming: Last-Event-ID (the browser's own reconnect), else last=.
    The hub keeps WEBAPI_LIVE_HISTORY recent events; a seq inside them
    is resumed with the missed events replayed, anything else (too old,
    from before a daemon restart, not a seq) starts with a resync. The
    hub itself resumes from its last seq whenever its connection to the
    daemon drops; a daemon that cannot resume (restarted) answers
    resync, which every open stream passes on.
  - Each event passes the stream's topics, then the request scope:
    eventInScope (internal/server/events.go) is the one place the
    scope from Server.requestScope decides it, for every event and
    every stream, so activity in a hidden chat never reaches a viewer.
    A stats.changed keeps only the visible chat and user ids and is
    dropped when none is left. Kinds the service does not know are
    dropped, since nobody can say who may see them. A member sees
    the events of no chat or user until grants exist.
  - Every WEBAPI_LIVE_RECHECK seconds an open stream checks its session
    again. Revoked, expired or the account gone: a "session" event with
    reason "revoked", then the stream closes. A different request
    scope: reason "scope_changed". The session event has no id, so the
    browser's Last-Event-ID stays the last real seq.
  - A stream gets a buffer of WEBAPI_LIVE_BUFFER events. A browser that
    reads slower than events arrive loses what is queued and gets a
    resync instead, so one slow reader costs bounded memory and slows
    nobody else.
  - Refusals, never an empty stream: 503 live_off when live updates
    are off (WEBAPI_LIVE=0 or no WEB_TGLOGGERD_API) or the daemon's
    stream is not connected; 503 unavailable with
    WEBAPI_LIVE_MAX_SUBSCRIBERS streams open. Both carry Retry-After.
    When the daemon's stream drops, open streams stay for 5 seconds in
    case it comes straight back, then close; their clients reconnect,
    are refused and poll until it is back.
  - On SIGTERM the hub stops first and every stream closes, so open
    streams do not hold the shutdown up.

## Routing /api/events through nginx

nginx buffers proxied answers by default, which would hold every event
back until a buffer fills. The service sends X-Accel-Buffering: no on
the stream, but give the path its own location next to /api/ as well,
so the settings do not depend on that header:

    location = /api/events {
        proxy_pass         http://10.0.88.7:8081;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header   Connection        "";
        proxy_set_header   Host              $host;
        proxy_set_header   X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        # Pass each frame on as it comes.
        proxy_buffering    off;
        proxy_cache        off;
        # The stream stays open; a keepalive comes every 15 seconds,
        # so this only ends streams that are really dead.
        proxy_read_timeout 1h;
    }

A list page subscribes to every user and group on screen, and its
request line can run to 16 KB and more. nginx's default
large_client_header_buffers (4 8k) refuses that with 414, so raise it
in the server (or http) block, where the directive belongs:

    large_client_header_buffers 4 32k;

The Go server's own header limit is the standard library's default,
1 MB, which leaves room. Serve the site over HTTP/2 (listen 443 ssl;
http2 on;): browsers allow six HTTP/1.1 connections per host and each
tab holds one for its stream, so with a few tabs open page requests
would queue.

# Pages

The React application's pages (web/app) have real URLs; the service
answers each with a complete HTML document, rendered by the render
service, and the same page data as JSON for navigation in the browser.
docs/design/web-ssr.md specifies both and the render protocol.

    GET /<page path>          the page's document (HTML)
    GET /api/page/<path>      the same page data as JSON (getPage)
    GET /app/assets/<file>    the client bundle's files

The page table (defaultPages in internal/server/pages.go) maps each
page path to its route id, shared with the React route registry, and
to a loader, which reads the page's props through the request scope
(Server.requestScope). A page whose data the service does not load
yet has no loader: its document is not served here, and /api/page
answers {"reload": true} for it, so the browser loads it in full from
the Drogon web. So far login, dashboard, user, user_history,
user_chat, group, group_admins, group_history, group_chat and message
have loaders (internal/server/pagedata.go, reading through
internal/logdb), and so do the list pages; not_found is the answer for
paths that are no page and for things the scope hides, 404 either way.

  - The list pages, users, groups, files, group_messages and
    private_messages (internal/server/lists.go), read their list state
    from the query string as the React list writes it (q, f with the
    filters as JSON, sort, dir, page, per) and run it through the
    advanced search (see Search), so the request scope limits their
    rows and totals the same way. A filter the page does not take is
    dropped and not echoed back; archive_total is only for a viewer
    who sees that whole list. The message lists order a search text
    by relevance unless a sort is asked for (sort "relevance").

  - /api/page/<path> takes the page path without its leading slash,
    its slashes plain or encoded as %2F, and the page's query string
    as its own. An empty path is the dashboard.
  - A signed-out visitor gets only the public pages: login, the three
    email link pages and the public chats of exposed groups. Any
    other path, a page or not, answers
    401 on /api/page and redirects a document request to
    /login?next=<path and query>; so does the chat of a group that is
    not exposed, whether it exists or not.
  - The password gate comes before the loader, for every page but the
    three link pages: a forced password change answers the
    password_change route at the URL asked for.
  - The page data's viewer carries the display settings in effect,
    the account's own or the site's (web/docs/settings.md); the
    document's <html> takes its lang and theme.
  - The document is one template (internal/render/document.go) for
    rendered and fallback pages; the page data is JSON with
    encoding/json's HTML escaping, and a signed-in document carries
    the CSRF token of the session cookie it was asked with.
  - The render service is reached over RENDER_SOCKET. A render waits
    at most RENDER_TIMEOUT; a refused connection or a timeout skips
    the service for RENDER_BACKOFF. Any failure serves the same
    document with empty markup (data-ssr="0"), which the browser
    renders from the embedded data.
  - The client bundle is WEBAPI_APP_DIST, read at start: the entry,
    styles and preloads come from its .vite/manifest.json, and its
    assets/ directory is served under /app/assets/ as immutable.
    The build id is the one the build recorded in the bundle's
    build.json (web/app README), which the render service was built
    with too; WEBAPI_APP_BUILD, when set, overrides it. The render
    service refuses another id (409), and the page falls back.
  - Signed-in answers are private, no-store; a public page asked for
    without a session cookie may be cached for a minute.

nginx sends a page path here once its loader is in place, as it does
/api/ and /files/.

In the compose deployment the render service is the "render" service,
and the render-build service builds both it and the client bundle;
web/app/README "Deployment" has the steps, including when this
service must be restarted.

internal/render/rendertest is a stand-in render service on a Unix
socket, used by the tests.

# Chats

GET /api/chats/{group|private}/{id}/messages answers a window of a
chat, oldest first, with has_older and has_newer: the newest messages,
or those before, after or around a message id (limit 60, at most 100).
It is the Drogon /v1/chats API (web/src/dao/Browse.cpp, chatHistory)
with the chat pages' message shape: the same cursors, replies resolved
within the chat, albums folded into one message (a window cut through
an album reads the rest of it, so it folds the same way in every
window), edits and link previews read in one query per window, media
with stored and fetchable as web/docs/media.md describes. The chat
page's loader reads its first window through the same code. after=0
is the oldest messages, and after_ts=<unix time> starts at the first
message sent then or later, as Drogon's after_ts did.

Each message carries what the Drogon chat view shows
(internal/server/chatmedia.go): how its media renders (image, video,
animation, lottie, audio, file), its size, duration, a sticker's
format and emoji, a voice note's waveform, an audio file's title and
performer, TDLib's blurred minithumbnail and a video's cover photo;
for album items their own message id, kind and deletion; each edit's
content type and media; the forward origin with its ids, the author's
signature, a chat posting as the sender, the service type, whether a
reply's target is logged or deleted. The detail comes from
content_extra (daemon migration 000034) and the cover from
telegram_message_files (000036); older rows fall back to what Drogon
reads, the content type and the file's extension. The kinds 000034
added to content_type are answered as unknown, with tdlib_type
naming them, until the chat view renders them. Both migrations must
have run before this service reads the logger's database.

The chat page's query also takes Drogon's links: ?after=, ?after_ts=
and ?limit=, and ?date= as a day or a day and time in the viewer's
timezone. Its header counts the members the archive has seen
(senders and admins), since the logger does not record Telegram's
count.

Signed-out visitors may read the chats of exposed groups
(telegram_public_groups), as the public chat page does, and get 401
for every other chat; a signed-in viewer sees a group its scope sees,
or an exposed one, and private chats when the scope sees them, and
gets 404 for the rest. A reply quotes another chat's message only to
a viewer that sees that chat.

GET /api/entities?users=<ids>&groups=<ids> (at most 200 each) answers
the summaries of those the scope sees and leaves the others out. A
viewer that does not see all users (a member) sees a user only where
the user appears in one of its visible groups, as a sender or an
admin, and then by name and photo only: no counts, contact name, bio,
history or other groups (logdb.VisibleUsers). Users and groups the
pages show come with the same rule.

# Search

    GET /api/search/{entity}   the advanced search of the Drogon web's
                               GET /v1/search/{entity}

The port of the Drogon web's advanced search, with the same
parameters, field registries and answers (web/docs/search-api.md, which
lists what differs). internal/logdb/search.go is the engine and
internal/logdb/search_entities.go the five entities (users, groups,
files, private_messages, group_messages), each with its displayed
columns and its condition of the request scope. It is under /api/
rather than at the Drogon path: nginx already sends /api/ here, and the
Drogon pages keep calling their own /v1/search, which answers a
signed-out request with a redirect and knows no request scope, until
they move.

  - The scope is in the SQL: the entity's condition (GroupFilter over
    the group column, or EXISTS over the messages of visible groups
    for users and files) is ANDed with the request's conditions, so
    rows, totals and EXPLAIN count only visible rows. Private messages
    are 404 without SeesPrivate. A member sees users by id, name,
    username, type and photo only; the other fields and columns are
    not listed and not accepted.
  - The total is exact when counting fits the 3-second statement
    limit; otherwise, and for group_messages without conditions, it is
    an estimate (total_exact false). The table's row estimate is used
    only when the scope adds no condition, so it never counts rows
    the viewer may not see.
  - debug=1 is for superadmins and admins.

    GET /api/chats/search      the groups and private chats a picker
                               offers (internal/logdb/chatsearch.go)

The groups the scope shows (GroupFilter) and, for a viewer who sees
private chats, the people the logging account has private messages
with, matched on title, name or username, busiest first, paged by an
opaque cursor. federation=<id> keeps the groups of that federation
(web/docs/accounts.md, Federations), read from the web's own schema
and passed to the logger's query as a list: 0 is Default, every group
in no other federation; an id that is no federation holds none.

# The request scope

Server.requestScope (internal/server/media.go) builds, once per
request, what it may see and do (internal/scope; docs/design/
web-accounts.md section 9), from the verified session: the account's
id, role and forced password change, as read from web_users on that
request. Every query over the logger's schema takes it and filters by
it, and every permission check asks it; there is no other mechanism.
Something outside it answers 404, a forbidden action on something
inside it 403.

So far it is built from the role and, for an admin, the federations it
administers (web/docs/accounts.md has the table): superadmins and
admins see every group, private chat and user; members see no group,
private chat or user until grants exist (web-member-access), and then
the users of their groups only by name and photo; admins may do all
but join chats and look up real names, and manage the members of
their federations (Scope.SeesAccount, AdministersFederation).
Restrictions and grants plug into the scope without changing its
callers; there is no gate on a missing email address
(decisions/2026-11-18-email-optional.md). Without a session
the scope sees nothing; a file token is its own authorization.

# Administration

The Drogon web's admin pages as JSON operations (openapi.yaml, tags
forwarding, join-chat, contacts and mcp) and page data; web/docs/
platform-forwarding.md, join-chat.md, contact-names.md and mcp.md
describe each, with what differs from the Drogon pages.

    /api/forwarding/telegram-discord[/{id}|/test]   webhooks
    /api/forwarding/discord-telegram[/{id}]         routes
    POST /api/join-chat/preview, /api/join-chat/join
    POST /api/users/{id}/lookup-name
    /api/mcp/groups[/{group_id}], /api/mcp/tokens[/{id}/revoke]

  - Each needs an ability of the request scope (Scope.Can): forward,
    join_chat, lookup_name, mcp. Without it the operation and its page
    do not exist (404); so does a configuration or group the scope
    hides, through the same queries as the lists (logdb.ChatFilter,
    GroupFilter). Every write takes the session's CSRF token.
  - Every change writes a web_audit row with who made it; chat_join
    and contact_lookup are worded as the Drogon pages word them, so
    both services' rows read alike.
  - Secrets are write-only: webhook URLs are answered with their token
    cut off, bots by name, MCP tokens once at creation; no answer, log
    line or audit row carries one. A webhook URL must be Discord's on
    save and on test (parseWebhook), so the service never posts
    elsewhere; the test's client follows no redirect.
  - The daemon is reached through internal/daemon (POST
    /v1/chats/resolve, /v1/chats/join, /v1/users/{id}/lookup-name);
    without WEB_TGLOGGERD_API those answer 502 daemon.
  - The forwarding tables and telegram_public_groups are the logger's;
    the service writes them through the read-only pool, whose account
    is granted DML on exactly those (web/docs/platform-forwarding.md
    and mcp.md, Grants), as the Drogon web does.
  - The federations a configuration or a joined group may go into
    are the existing ones the viewer administers (fileFederations);
    the accounts and federations pages take their federation data from
    the federation helpers (federationprops.go). Filing a joined group
    comes with join-chat-federations.
  - Every create operation records the account creating the resource
    as its owner (internal/server/ownership.go, over the helpers of
    internal/webdb/ownership.go: Owners, OwnerOf, OwnedBy, SetOwner,
    TransferOwner, DropResource, AssignUnowned), and every delete
    drops it; each change is audited (owner_set, owner_transfer,
    resource_drop). The forwarding lists answer the owner through
    Server.ownership; a resource without one counts as the
    superadmins' and is answered as shared. The configurations'
    federation is not stored yet (Default) and the transfers are
    planned (forwarding-per-user).

# MCP

    POST /mcp     one JSON-RPC 2.0 message of the Model Context Protocol
    GET  /mcp     405, Allow: POST (no server-to-client stream)
    DELETE /mcp   405, Allow: POST (no sessions)

The port of the Drogon web's MCP endpoint (McpController, the gwmcp
library and web/src/mcp): the same path, transport, methods, tools,
arguments and answers, byte for byte, and the same bearer tokens.
web/docs/mcp.md describes the behaviour for both; internal/mcp is the
engine (JSON-RPC framing, initialize, ping, tools/list, tools/call, the
fourteen telegram_* tools, the filter tree, the word lists) and
internal/server/mcp.go the transport. No third-party module: JSON-RPC
over HTTP is a few lines of the standard library.

  - Tokens: "Authorization: Bearer tgmcp_<64 hex>", else ?key=, looked
    up in web_mcp_tokens by SHA-256 (web migration 000003). Missing,
    malformed, unknown, revoked or a disabled account's: 401 with a
    JSON-RPC error and no WWW-Authenticate header (web/docs/mcp.md says
    why). A use stamps last_used_at. The route takes no session and no
    CSRF token, and the token's account and its role do not limit the
    tools: what they read is decided by the exposure list alone, as in
    the Drogon web.
  - Exposure: the tools read only the groups on the curated list
    telegram_public_groups, never one that merely has a public username.
    The message tools go through one gate, msgGate (internal/mcp/
    gate.go), which binds the listed ids as constants; the group tools
    carry the list as a condition of their statement; the user tools are
    not limited to it, as in the Drogon web. Nothing in internal/mcp
    names the private messages table (a test checks).
  - The two query-plan traps of web/docs/mcp.md are avoided the same
    way: message scans choose their ids from the message table alone and
    join the display columns onto at most limit rows (runMessageQuery),
    and recent messages are one indexed walk per exposed group, merged
    (listRecent).
  - Answers are written by internal/mcp's own encoder, which spells
    them as the Drogon web's JSON library does (sorted keys, no HTML
    escaping, "25.0" for a whole double), so both services give the
    same bytes. internal/server/testdata/mcp/drogon.json holds the
    Drogon web's answers to the requests of cases.json on the "mcp"
    fixture, captured from it; TestMCPDrogonAnswers wants the same from
    this service. The one known difference is the text of a JSON parse
    error, which quotes each JSON parser's own message.
  - The word lists of telegram_popular_words are the Drogon web's
    (stopwords.go, generated from web/src/mcp/Stopwords.hpp, keeps that
    file's MIT attribution; noisewords.go is NoiseWords.hpp); a test
    compares them with the C++ headers.
  - At most MCP_THREADS tool calls run at once; the rest wait for a
    turn, so slow queries hold at most that many connections of the
    read-only pool. Every statement carries the 5-second
    MAX_EXECUTION_TIME of the Drogon code.
  - MCP_LOG_REQUESTS=1 logs the shape of each request (whether a
    credential came, never its value; Origin, MCP-Protocol-Version,
    User-Agent) as an "mcp-trace" line. The request log line never has
    the query string, so a ?key= does not reach the log.

## Routing /mcp through nginx

While Drogon serves /mcp, nothing changes. To move it, add an exact
location next to the /api/ one, with the same upstream:

    location = /mcp {
        proxy_pass         http://10.0.88.7:8081;
        proxy_http_version 1.1;
        proxy_set_header   Connection        "";
        proxy_set_header   Host              $host;
        proxy_set_header   X-Real-IP         $remote_addr;
        proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-For   $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;
        proxy_set_header   X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;
        # A tool call may wait for a turn and then run statements of up
        # to 5 seconds each.
        proxy_read_timeout 120s;
        # Optional: keeps a ?key= token out of the access log.
        access_log         off;
    }

The query string, ?key= included, is passed on as it is. "location =
/mcp" matches the path exactly, so /mcp/ and /mcp-admin stay where they
are. The default log format records the request line, query string
included; a format without $request, $request_uri and $args can keep
access_log on.
The Drogon route stays in place, so moving back is removing the
location. Both services read the same tokens and the same exposure
list, so a client notices no change, and WEB_PUBLIC_URL (or
TG_DISCORD_PUBLIC_URL), MCP_THREADS, MCP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS and
MCP_LOG_REQUESTS come from the same .env.

Before the switch, scripts/mcp_compare.py sends the same requests to
both services, one right after the other, and reports every answer
that is not the same bytes: the transport and token refusals, and every
tool on ids it finds through the endpoints (the exposed groups, their
senders, recent and edited messages). It needs a live token in
MCP_TOKEN and both services' /mcp URLs:

    MCP_TOKEN=tgmcp_... scripts/mcp_compare.py \
        --a http://<drogon>/mcp --b http://<webapi>/mcp

# Account email

    POST /api/account/email          change the address (verification link)
    POST /api/account/email/verify   use a verification link
    POST /api/password/set           use a set-password link
    POST /api/password/forgot        ask for a reset link
    POST /api/accounts/{id}/resend-invite

web/docs/email.md describes the flows and openapi.yaml the operations
(Gates and Email links in its description). In short:

  - Links are <site>/password/set#token=<token> and
    <site>/account/email/verify#token=<token>, single-use, expiring,
    stored as the SHA-256 of the token (web_email_links). The token is
    in the fragment and the pages post it only when the person acts,
    so the pages' GETs consume nothing.
  - Email is optional, for each account and for the service
    (decisions/2026-11-18-email-optional.md): no account is asked for
    an address. Links and notices go out only when the account has an
    address and mail is set up; otherwise nothing is sent and nothing
    waits for mail.
  - A new account gets a typed or a generated temporary password and
    must change it at its first sign-in; with "invite": true, a
    set-password link instead. A reset sends a link to a verified
    address when mail is on, else gives a temporary password. Setting
    a password from a link verifies the address it went to. The
    forgotten-password form answers 202 whatever it is asked, sends
    only to a verified address, and is throttled per client and per
    account.
  - The security notices (password, address, new device, disabled,
    role) go to the verified address only. Texts come from
    internal/i18n (mail.*), in the account's language and time zone.
  - internal/mail sends through the SMTP relay of WEBAPI_SMTP_*: queued
    in memory, never blocking a request, retried for about a day on a
    temporary failure, logged without the bodies. Tests use
    internal/mail/mailtest, an SMTP server on a loopback port.

# Layout

    openapi.yaml           The API description (see The API contract).
    go.mod                 Module tgloggerd/webapi and its requirements.
    go.sum                 Checksums of the required modules.
    cmd/webapi/            main: configuration, the two pools, the HTTP
                           server, shutdown on SIGTERM.
    internal/config/       Settings from the environment.
    internal/server/       Routes and handlers.
    internal/auth/         Sessions, CSRF, file tokens, passwords, the
                           login throttle, as the Drogon web does them.
    internal/daemon/       Client of the daemon's internal API.
    internal/live/         Live updates: the daemon's event stream, the
                           hub that fans it out (livetest: a fake
                           daemon stream for tests).
    internal/webdb/        The web's own schema: accounts and audit.
    internal/logdb/        Reads of the logger's schema, each taking the
                           request scope, and the writes the web is
                           granted there (forwarding, MCP exposure).
    internal/scope/        The request scope (what a request may see).
    internal/settings/     An account's preferences: the known keys,
                           their validation, the site defaults.
    internal/i18n/         The user-facing texts by language, one
                           catalogue per language (catalog/<lang>.json).
    internal/mcp/          The MCP engine: JSON-RPC, the tools, the
                           filter tree, the tokens, the exposure gate.
    internal/media/        The rules files are served by: store path,
                           names and types, ranges, the fetch policy,
                           the TDLib path check.
    internal/render/       The render service's client, the client
                           assets and the page document.
    internal/testdb/       A database per test, and the dummy-data
                           fixtures (see Tests).
    internal/mail/         Outbound mail: the SMTP sender and its
                           queue (mailtest: an SMTP server for tests).
    vendor/                The third-party modules, committed.
    build/                 Build output (git-ignored).

Routes use the standard library's ServeMux with method and path
patterns ("GET /api/health"). Add a third-party router only with a
reason it cannot give.

# The module path names no host

The module path is `tgloggerd/webapi`, and the service's own imports are
`tgloggerd/webapi/...`. It must never start with a repository host
and account, the way third-party paths do: the code is the same wherever a copy of the
repository is hosted, and moving it changes nothing. Third-party modules
keep their upstream paths; those are the only host names under webapi/,
in go.mod, go.sum and vendor/. To check:

    grep -rnE '[a-z0-9-]+\.(com|org|net|io|dev)/' webapi \
        --exclude-dir=vendor --exclude=go.sum

must print only the third-party require lines of go.mod and the
imports of those modules.

# Build and run

In the compose deployment the "webapi" service (container
tgloggerd-webapi) runs `run.sh webapi`, which builds this directory into
webapi/build/webapi with the toolchain pinned in the Dockerfile and
execs it. The build uses only vendor/ (GOFLAGS=-mod=vendor, GOPROXY=off),
so it never reaches the network. The container is not published to the
host; nginx reaches it on its tgnet address (see web/README.web).

By hand, from this directory, with the same Go version:

    GOFLAGS=-mod=vendor GOPROXY=off go build -o build/webapi ./cmd/webapi
    go vet ./...
    go test ./...

The tests that need MySQL (the sign-in and media tests in
internal/server, and every new one) take a database each from
internal/testdb, on the server WEBAPI_TEST_MYSQL_DSN names, and are
skipped without it; see Tests. The media tests run the daemon as a fake
on a loopback port the system picks.
The live update tests (internal/live, events_test.go in
internal/server) need no database: they run livetest's fake daemon
stream on a loopback port the system picks, and run in parallel.

The service applies no migrations: the web service owns the web schema
and the daemon the logger's.

# One-off commands

With arguments the binary runs a command and exits instead of serving,
with the same configuration from the environment (it needs the two
pools' settings, and WEB_APP_KEY like the service). Exit status 0 done,
1 failed or refused, 2 a usage or configuration error.

    webapi assign-owner <username>

gives every resource without an owner (MCP exposed groups, MCP tokens,
Telegram bots, Telegram to Discord webhooks, Discord to Telegram
routes; Discord bots once their table exists) to that account, which
must be an active superadmin (web/docs/accounts.md, Ownership); MCP
tokens go to the account that minted each instead. It is
idempotent: resources that have an owner are left alone, so a second
run assigns nothing, and a later run assigns only what was made
without an owner since (by the Drogon pages, say). Each resource
assigned gets an owner_set row in web_audit, by no account, with the
owner as its target. It prints what it assigned by type:

    Owner: superadmin (id 1)
    mcp_group     2 assigned: -1001000000010 -1001000000003; 1 owned already
    mcp_token     1 assigned to their creators: 2; 1 owned already
    ...
    discord_bot   not stored yet
    Assigned 8 resources.

In the compose deployment, run it in the webapi container once web
migration 000008 is applied and the account exists:

    docker compose exec webapi webapi/build/webapi assign-owner superadmin

# Tests

Every feature ships with its tests, and the tests run in parallel. One
command runs all of the web's checks, with only Docker on the host:

    scripts/test-web.sh                 # webapi and web/app
    scripts/test-web.sh --go-only -run TestMedia -count=1

For webapi it starts the test MySQL of docker-compose.test.yml (its own
compose project, tmpfs, no published port), runs gofmt, go vet and
go test -race ./... in the pinned Go image on that project's internal
network, and stops the MySQL again if it started it. A MySQL that was
already running is used and left running, so for several runs at once
start it first:

    docker compose -f docker-compose.test.yml up -d --wait
    scripts/test-web.sh --go-only & scripts/test-web.sh --go-only; wait
    docker compose -f docker-compose.test.yml down

## A database per test

A test that needs MySQL takes a database of its own from
internal/testdb, with the logger's (testdb.Logger) or the web's
(testdb.Web) migrations applied and, if it asks, dummy data loaded:

    func TestSomething(t *testing.T) {
        t.Parallel()
        ro := testdb.New(t, testdb.Logger, "sample") // fixtures/logger/sample.sql
        app := testdb.New(t, testdb.Web, "accounts") // fixtures/web/accounts.sql
        h := New(Deps{RO: ro.DB, App: app.DB, ...})
        ...
    }

The database is dropped when the test ends. db.Load(t, "name") loads
more fixtures later; db.Name and db.DSN name the database. Rows a test
adds are its own, so a test never empties a table and may call
t.Parallel(). Without WEBAPI_TEST_MYSQL_DSN (a DSN without a database
name, of an account that may create and drop databases, such as
root:test@tcp(mysql:3306)/) New skips the test.

The migrations are applied once per change of the files, into a
template database (test_tpl_<schema>_<hash>) built under a MySQL
named lock, so test binaries running at once, from `go test ./...` or
from separate runs, share it; each test's database is made from the
template's table definitions. A new migration needs nothing more.

The fixtures are invented data, nothing from production, following the
named samples of the app's stand-in (web/mockup/src/data). The header
of each file lists the ids it uses, so a test can add rows beside them
without clashing. A fixture that changes must keep the package's own
tests (internal/testdb) passing; they check, among other things, that
the message counters agree with the messages. Add a fixture as a new
file when a test needs data the existing ones do not hold, rather than
changing rows other tests rely on.

To run the tests from the host instead, publish the port:

    docker compose -f docker-compose.test.yml \
        -f docker-compose.test-port.yml up -d --wait
    WEBAPI_TEST_MYSQL_DSN='root:test@tcp(127.0.0.1:33306)/' go test -race ./...

# Configuration

The database settings are the Drogon web's, with the same defaults, so
one .env serves both (see .env.example):

    WEB_DB_HOST, WEB_DB_PORT     the MySQL server; fall back to
                                 TG_DB_HOST, TG_DB_PORT
    WEB_DB_RO_NAME, _USER, _PASSWORD, _CONNS
                                 the read-only pool over the logger's
                                 schema
    WEB_DB_APP_NAME, _USER, _PASSWORD, _CONNS
                                 the read-write pool over the web's own
                                 schema

It reads the Drogon web's authentication and daemon settings as well,
with the same meaning, since the two must agree:

    WEB_APP_KEY                  required; the service does not start
                                 without a usable one. Base64 of 16 to
                                 64 bytes.
    WEB_SESSION_TIMEOUT          session lifetime in seconds, default
                                 43200; 0 or less never expires
    WEB_SECURE_COOKIE            "0" drops the cookies' Secure flag
    WEB_LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS, WEB_LOGIN_WINDOW
                                 the login throttle, default 5 failures
                                 per address and username per 900
                                 seconds
    WEB_LOGIN_MAX_ATTEMPTS_PER_ACCOUNT
                                 failures per account from all
                                 addresses in the same window, default
                                 20; 0 turns this cap off. Anyone who
                                 knows a name can use it up and lock
                                 the account's logins for the window
    WEB_TGLOGGERD_API            the daemon's internal API as
                                 http://host:port; empty or malformed
                                 leaves the client off
    TG_FETCH_TIMEOUT_SECS        the daemon's fetch limit; a fetch waits
                                 10 seconds longer
    WEB_STORAGE_DIR              the file store, default
                                 data/storage/files; must be the
                                 daemon's TG_STORAGE_DIR
    TG_DATA_DIR                  the daemon's data directory; partial
                                 files are served only from its tdlib/
                                 (none when unset)
    TG_MAX_STORE_FILE_SIZE       the daemon's per-file store cap,
                                 default 1073741824; 0 = none. A request
                                 without a session fetches only files
                                 under it.

and the Drogon web's MCP settings, with the same meaning:

    WEB_PUBLIC_URL               the site's public base URL, for absolute
                                 /files/<token> links in MCP answers;
                                 falls back to TG_DISCORD_PUBLIC_URL.
                                 Neither: the links are left out
    MCP_THREADS                  MCP tool calls run at once, default 2,
                                 1 to 16
    MCP_ALLOWED_ORIGINS          browser origins allowed to call /mcp,
                                 comma-separated, or *; empty allows
                                 any (a token is required anyway)
    MCP_LOG_REQUESTS             "1" logs the shape of /mcp requests

and its own:

    WEBAPI_LISTEN_ADDR   default 127.0.0.1
    WEBAPI_LISTEN_PORT   default 8081
    WEBAPI_LOG_LEVEL     debug, info (default), warn or error
    WEBAPI_LIVE          "0" turns live updates (GET /api/events) off;
                         they also need WEB_TGLOGGERD_API
    WEBAPI_LIVE_MAX_SUBSCRIBERS   open event streams at most, default 256
    WEBAPI_LIVE_BUFFER   events queued per stream before it gets a
                         resync instead, default 256
    WEBAPI_LIVE_HISTORY  recent events kept for streams that reconnect,
                         default 4096
    WEBAPI_LIVE_RECHECK  seconds between two session checks of an open
                         stream, default 30
    WEBAPI_APP_DIST      the client bundle, default web/app/dist/client
    WEBAPI_APP_BUILD     its build id; default the one in its
                         build.json, or dev when it has none
    RENDER_SOCKET        the render service's socket, default
                         /run/tgloggerd-render/render.sock
    RENDER_TIMEOUT       milliseconds a render may take, default 500
    RENDER_BACKOFF       milliseconds the render service is skipped
                         after it failed to answer, default 2000
    WEBAPI_DEFAULT_TIMEZONE, WEBAPI_DEFAULT_DATE_FORMAT,
    WEBAPI_DEFAULT_LANG, WEBAPI_DEFAULT_THEME
                         the site's display settings for accounts that
                         set none, default Asia/Jakarta, 24h, en and
                         system (web/docs/settings.md); a value the
                         preference does not take stops the service
    WEBAPI_SMTP_HOST     the SMTP relay of account mail; unset turns
                         mail off: no links or notices are sent, and
                         first passwords and resets are temporary
                         passwords
    WEBAPI_SMTP_PORT     default 587, or 465 with tls
    WEBAPI_SMTP_SECURITY starttls (default), tls, or none (a relay on
                         the same host only)
    WEBAPI_SMTP_USER, WEBAPI_SMTP_PASSWORD
                         AUTH PLAIN; unset sends without
    WEBAPI_SMTP_FROM     the sender; required with a relay
    WEB_PUBLIC_URL       the site the links point to, falling back to
                         TG_DISCORD_PUBLIC_URL; an http(s) URL is
                         required with a relay
    WEBAPI_INVITE_LINK_TTL, WEBAPI_RESET_LINK_TTL, WEBAPI_VERIFY_LINK_TTL
                         seconds a link works: invitations and admin
                         resets (default 259200), forgotten-password
                         resets (3600), address verifications (86400)

An empty value counts as unset. A malformed number stops the service at
start. Logs are structured (log/slog, key=value) on stderr, one line per
request.

On SIGTERM (docker stop) or SIGINT the service stops accepting
connections, lets requests in flight finish for up to 8 seconds, closes
the pools and exits 0.

# Updating the vendored modules

Third-party modules are few and each needs a reason. Adding or updating
one is the only step that uses the network, and the result is committed:

    go get <module>@<version>    # or edit go.mod
    go mod tidy
    go mod vendor
    go build ./... && go vet ./...

Commit go.mod, go.sum and vendor/ together, and add any new module and
its license to the third-party list in LICENSE. Run it with the same Go
version as the image, with GOFLAGS unset (-mod=vendor would stop go get).
The go directive in go.mod is the oldest Go the code and its modules
need, not the image's version; raising the image's toolchain does not
change it.
